const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Marketing Communication Content Development","serviceType":"Promotional content development","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/marketing-communication-content-development-service/","description":"Healthcare and life science marketing content written to the code that governs each market — ABPI 2024, EFPIA, FDA OPDP, PAAB, TGA.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What types of marketing communication content do you develop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Website and product copy, company profiles and brochures, sales aids and detail material, case studies and customer stories, campaign and launch material, social and digital copy with the moderation policy that goes with it, press releases and media material, email campaigns, and congress and conference material. Plus two things that are not copy and are frequently the better purchase: the substantiation file that documents every claim against the finding supporting it, and an independent review of material written by an agency or in-house before it reaches your own reviewers. Across pharmaceuticals, biotechnology, medical devices and diagnostics, health services, and the corporate and B2B organisations selling into them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why did you remove COPE and ICMJE from your compliance section?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because they do not govern a single thing this service produces. COPE and the ICMJE govern journal publication: authorship of research papers, editorial decisions, corrections, retractions, publication ethics. Website copy, a brochure, a sales aid and a campaign are none of those. What governs them is the promotional code of every market the material reaches — the ABPI Code in the UK, EFPIA and national codes in the EU, FDA rules enforced by OPDP plus the PhRMA and AdvaMed Codes in the US, PAAB in Canada, the Therapeutic Goods Advertising Code in Australia, the PMD Act in Japan. The detail worth knowing is that this is rarely ignorance. A supplier’s parent page will often name all of these correctly while the child page selling the work names none of them. The knowledge is on the site; it just never reaches the page where it matters, which is worth checking one level down before you buy."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can we advertise our prescription medicine to patients?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In almost no market. The European Union prohibits advertising prescription-only medicines to the general public under Directive 2001/83/EC; the United Kingdom prohibits it under the Human Medicines Regulations 2012; Australia prohibits it under its Therapeutic Goods Advertising Code. The United States is one of a very small number of countries that permits it at all — New Zealand is the other example usually cited. Where it is permitted, it is heavily ruled: the FDA’s final rule requiring the major statement in DTC television and radio advertisements to be presented in a clear, conspicuous and neutral manner was published on 21 November 2023, took effect on 20 May 2024 and had a compliance date of 20 November 2024. What is available elsewhere is disease-awareness material that does not identify the product — and that line is considerably harder to hold than to describe, because a campaign naming no medicine but recognisably about one is precisely the case a regulator examines. If that is the route, the code gets checked before the creative."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can we say our product is better than the alternative?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only if you have the study that shows it. Comparative claims are permitted in most markets and are the claims most often challenged, usually by the competitor you named. Three things have to hold: the comparison must be fair and like for like on a clinically meaningful measure rather than a favourable subgroup; it must be substantiated by data capable of supporting it, which in medicines advertising generally means a head-to-head trial rather than a cross-trial comparison; and it must still be true, because a comparison that was accurate when the copy was written can become a live compliance problem in an asset nobody is looking at. The failure we see most often is a superiority claim resting on a non-inferiority trial. A non-inferiority result does not support a superiority claim in any of these markets, no phrasing rescues it, and a competitor’s medical team will say so in a complaint that names your company."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do the rules change for social media?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and assuming they do is the mistake this category makes most often. Three consequences. Space is not a defence: if a claim requires prescribing information or a safety statement, a character limit does not excuse its absence — the answer is that the claim does not go in that format, not that the safety information gets shorter. Reach is the test, not intent: material aimed at healthcare professionals on an open channel is available to the public, and in markets that prohibit public promotion of prescription medicines that is the problem regardless of who it was written for. And interaction creates content: a company account that likes, shares or replies can adopt a third party’s claim as its own, which is why the moderation policy is a compliance document rather than a community one. On correcting misinformation about your own products online, the FDA issued revised draft guidance on 8 July 2024, replacing its 2014 draft."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you make sure it aligns with our brand voice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your style guide where you have one, your claim library, your terminology, and two or three pieces you were genuinely happy with as reference — which is more useful than a guide, because a guide describes the intention and a piece shows the result. Where you have several style guides that disagree we will tell you, since that is usually why material from different teams does not read as one organisation. One constraint worth being honest about in this category: in regulated material, voice has less room than it does elsewhere. Much of a compliant sales aid is determined by the code and the labelling, and the distinctive part of the brand has to live in the space that is left. A supplier promising a strong brand voice in a piece that is 60% obligatory content is describing something that will not survive your own review."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you create multilingual content?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is not translation. A translated asset has to be re-checked against the code of the market it is going to, because the codes differ on what may be claimed, what must accompany it and whether the audience may be addressed at all — a national association code in the EU is frequently stricter than EFPIA, which is why “EU-compliant” is not a meaningful status for an asset. The approved labelling also differs by market, so a claim that matches the label in one country can sit outside it in another. Producing one global asset and localising the disclaimers is the standard way a compliant piece in one market becomes an unlawful one in another. We quote market versions as separate deliverables from a shared evidence base. It looks more expensive at quote and is usually cheaper by the second review cycle."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will you deal with our medical, legal and regulatory review?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We write for it, we attend it, and we do not try to shorten it by any other means. Material arrives with the substantiation file attached — every claim, the reference, the specific finding relied on, the population and the labelling version — which turns a review meeting from a debate into a check and is the single biggest thing that reduces cycles. We work to your templates, your job-code system and your document management rather than introducing a second process alongside your validated one. What we will not do is help you around that function. It exists to stop the company doing something expensive, it should be independent of the people producing the material, and a supplier offering to route past it is offering you a liability rather than a service. Where your reviewers disagree with us, they are right by definition; where we think a claim is unsafe, you get that in writing first."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I see a sample before commissioning?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and there is a complete one on this page — a claim and substantiation file for a prescription-medicine content set across three markets, with the product, the indication, the client and every figure changed. Open it above and read the whole thing, including the parts that cost the client something: a campaign line withdrawn because a non-inferiority trial cannot support a superiority claim, two claims removed for resting on cross-trial comparison, and a recommendation to build three market versions where one had been budgeted. If you want something closer to your own work, ask and we will build one around a set like yours. Two caveats. Client work is confidential, so any sample is redacted or composite — a supplier who sends you another company’s actual pre-launch material has told you how they will treat yours. And a sample shows method; it cannot show whether the writer understands your product, which is what the free claim check is for."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Scientific communication","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/marketing-communication-content-development-service/"}]}];

